.TH SPLITTEST 1
.SH NAME
splittest \- regression test for split(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B splittest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Splittest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/split.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(args) ,
and checks it against golden output for: default sequential splitting,
.BR -e 's
capture-group and no-capture-group forms, that a line before the first
pattern match is silently dropped,
.BR -x ,
.BR -i 's
effect on both matching and the resulting captured filename's case, and
that
.B -s
only affects capture-group-derived filenames, not sequential ones.
.PP
Unlike most
.B p9/cmd
tests,
.IR split 's
whole job is creating real files, so this test drives it entirely
with real files: it writes a real input file, calls
.BR run 's
seam with a real trailing file operand, reads the resulting output
files back, and removes every file it created (input and output alike)
when done, whether or not anything failed.
.PP
Every case was checked against
.BR plan9port 's
real
.I split
binary first (this tree's local checkout \(em see
.IR split (1)'s
BUGS for why plan9port, not 9front, is the reference for this specific
file), not assumed from reading
.BR split.b .
.PP
Prints one line per failure to standard error, a final
.IR N " passed, " N " failed"
summary to standard output, and exits with a non-nil status if
anything failed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/splittest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR split (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I split
in-process via its
.B run
seam, which is exactly
.IR split 's
own post-argv0 argument handling \(em so it does exercise real option
parsing and real file creation, but not
.IR split 's
own
.B init
or its standard-input (no
.I file
argument) path, neither of which
.B run
goes through.
